refully: show front-only details only when the product front is visible; if the brief shows the back, side, top, or an angled view where those details would not be visible, omit them instead of moving them onto the wrong face.\\n\\nThe attached product photos show the real product — reproduce it faithfully.\\n\\nDesign requirements (apply to this ad):\\n- Follow the instructions by the director.\\n- Make the ad design, layout, typography on-branded, but should look premium, editorial-like.\\n- Establish a clear visual hierarchy: one dominant focal point the eye lands on\\n  first, then the supporting message, then the CTA.\\n- Keep strong figure/ground contrast. If text sits over imagery, place a scrim or\\n  overlay behind it so it stays fully legible.\\n- Place the focal subject on a rule-of-thirds line; leave generous whitespace and\\n  avoid clutter.\\n- Use a limited palette anchored to the brand colors (~60-30-10), reserving one\\n  accent color for the call to action.\\n- Keep 20% space all around the creative for the ad to look cleaner\\n- Group and align elements cleanly (Gestalt); use at most two type styles.\",\"status\":\"generating\",\"createdBy\":\"f5dfb377-dac7-46a6-aea3-a4e04cb45e54\",\"metadata\":{\"brief\":\"Using the PAS framework to target top of funnel discovery, this static 1:1 ad addresses the Problem Solver archetype by dramatizing the daily friction of tired, dehydrated skin. The visual hero is a bold, split-screen graphic: the left side is a muted, desaturated Mist Gray (#f2f4f7) representing dull, lifeless skin texture, while the right side is a high-energy, vibrant coral-pink splash of the dissolved collagen drink next to the clean white 'glow' tub, representing instant revival. The high-impact on-image headline is written in clean, medium-weight Circular Std and reads: 'Tired of dull morning skin?' A pill-shaped accent tag at the top in Primary Brand Orange (#ff5000) highlights 'Korean Marine Collagen.' The soft call to action at the bot